<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vicenza</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vicetia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vicenza is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, between the Berici Hills and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Ancient Vicetia was a town of the Veneti and later a Roman municipium. It came under Venetian rule in 1404 and is famous for the buildings of the 16th-century architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio">Andrea Palladio</a>, which with his villas in the Veneto form a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vicenza.jpg" alt="Vicenza" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: collection by DanieleDF1995 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_Vicenza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
